  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Explain why $\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x-3}$ is not defined when $x=3$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "When $x=3$, the original denominator is zero, so the expression is undefined. The simplified form $x+3$ is equivalent to the original only for $x\\ne3$.",
      "worked": "At $x=3$, the denominator is $3-3=0$. Division by zero is undefined.\n\nFor $x\\ne3$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}&=\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}\\\\&=x+3.\\end{align*}$$\nCancelling $x-3$ is valid only when $x-3\\ne0$, so the original expression remains undefined at $x=3$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 20
  },
